WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.090
"Don't Look Up" is a truly stellar satire﻿
of the complications that can unexpectedly

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.440
arise at the end of the world.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.730
The film follows a pair of brainy astronomers,
Randall Mindy and Kate Dibiasky, as they accidentally

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.740
discover a planet-killing comet on a direct
collision course with Earth.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.090
After the scientists are basically blown off
by the ratings-obsessed president, they have

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.000
no choice but to go straight to the public.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.480
Co-written and directed by Adam McKay, the
film features an all-star cast of actors who

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.530
fully embody their characters as they grapple
with some wild, but alarmingly realistic,

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.840
reactions to this doom-and-gloom news.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.350
There are also quite a few things in the film
that require a particularly keen eye to spot.

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:46.089
Let's take a closer look at some of the small
details you might have missed in "Don't Look

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:47.089
Up."

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:51.569
But don't look any further if you haven't
watched the film; there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:51.569 --> 00:00:56.780
Few rooms have ever been as precisely recreated
for the screen as the Oval Office.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.199
This iconic centerpiece of the White House
is a mainstay of political cinema, and that's

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:03.180
no exception in "Don't Look Up."

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.470
Sure, it takes Randall, Kate, and NASA career
official Dr. Teddy Oglethorpe quite a while

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:13.570
to actually get an audience with President
Orlean, but once they do take a seat in the

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.189
office, it's a familiar sight.

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:18.940
There are some specific details scattered
throughout the film's Oval Office that you

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.480
might need to hit the pause button to catch.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.729
President Orlean has clearly given it her
own decorative spin, and her particular choices

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:28.070
are quite telling about her character.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.490
Most prominently, you might notice a greenish
hue to the curtains and other accessories

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.490
in the room.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.640
This is a clear nod to President Orlean's
preoccupation with money.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.399
Her library in those iconic built-in shelves
is also self-aggrandizing.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:46.290
Instead of outfitting the space with some
inspirational texts from the great minds of

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.649
the world, she's stocked the shelves with
her own book, "How to Manage Your Money Even

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:51.850
When You Have None."

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.070
It should be no surprise, then, that when
confronted with a potential cash grab, she

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:01.180
opts to put literally all of humanity in danger
for the sake of her own greed.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:02.659
"Let's bottom line this.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:05.399
What's this gonna cost me, you know?

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:07.539
What's the ask here?"

00:02:07.539 --> 00:02:11.540
Orlean has also chosen to line her office
with the official portraits of presidents

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.709
with controversial histories.

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:16.730
She also has a photo of Dick Cheney on her
desk.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:21.470
It makes total sense in the film world, since
they probably run in the same political circles,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:26.850
but it's also a bit of a wink to McKay's own
previous political dramedy, "Vice."

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.349
According to director Adam McKay, the theme
of "Don't Look Up" is the sensible lead characters

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:34.120
taking an unexpected trip through Hades.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.099
At least one of the choices of costume designer
Susan Matheson reflects that concept.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:43.980
President Orlean's fashion sense bounces between
red, white, and blue, indicating her played-up

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.970
patriotism throughout the story.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:51.120
And her chief of staff, who's also her son,
carries a Birkin handbag as an indication

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.050
of their family's wealth – a prop suggestion
that hilariously came from Jonah Hill himself.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.519
Most tellingly, when our leads first meet
her, she's cloaked in fiery red, indicating

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:05.049
their descent into a hellish world of madness.

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:09.550
Audiences may also notice that both Randall
and Kate endure something of a fashion overhaul

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:10.550
themselves.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.189
The no-nonsense Kate eventually puts away
her casual sweaters and combat boots in favor

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:20.050
of some business-ready blouses and jackets
in hopes of being taken more seriously.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.799
Not that it makes any difference in the end,
of course.

00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:27.230
Meanwhile, after a lifetime spent observing
far-away galaxies, Randall becomes something

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.220
of a star in his own right.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.689
After impressing the co-hosts of "The Rip"
with his on-air work and freshened-up look,

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:36.180
he becomes a mainstay of the screen scene.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.920
His suits become far more refined and fitted
as he makes his way from one media appearance

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:41.920
to another.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.220
When he finally decides to give up the pointless
limelight and head home to Michigan with the

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.890
proverbial hat in hand, he shifts back to
his unassuming outerwear and plaids while

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:54.720
Kate returns to her own no-fuss knit sweaters.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.630
As you watch "Don't Look Up," keep an eye
out for small references to a pretty grim

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:00.700
concept: extinction.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.610
Randall, Kate, and Dr. Oglethorpe might hold
out hope to the bitter end for humanity to

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.989
overcome their apocalyptic obstacle, but there
are signs scattered throughout the film that

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:13.549
they're all just as doomed as everyone else.

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:17.780
For example, when Randall and Kate first decide
to turn to the media for help in spreading

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.610
their message, they pass by someone dressed
as a dinosaur passing out flyers in the street.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:26.970
You might also notice a polar bear with an
umbrella precariously located in the liquor

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.639
store where Kate works.

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:33.520
Adam McKay wanted viewers to connect the comet
with climate change, and visuals like this

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.830
do a lot to hammer that home.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.080
These are just some of the visual nods to
ultimate catastrophe sprinkled throughout

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.890
the action, so keep your eyes peeled for these
and other dark clues that can be found in

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.240
the background.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.610
Almost every actor in "Don't Look Up" goes
through a bit of a physical transformation

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.500
to become their character.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.300
Just look at Jennifer Lawrence's sharp bangs,
red coif, and facial piercings as Kate.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.810
Leonardo DiCaprio's Randall sports wild facial
hair and Meryl Streep's President Orlean has

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.970
coiled locks and cat-eye glasses.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:10.780
However, no one is quite as unrecognizable
in their role as Mark Rylance is as tech mogul

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.440
Peter Isherwell.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.960
Isherwell is an off-putting amalgamation of
many figures from the tech industry's past

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:18.419
and present.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:22.669
Costume designer Susan Matheson decided that
since the character was poised to be anywhere

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:27.750
between 40 and 80 years old, so she wanted
to maintain that ambiguity with fake teeth,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.610
hair, and other body modifications available
to a billionaire of his stature.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:37.190
He's also fully committed to his profession,
as indicated by his choice of sweater during

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:42.940
his big "Liif" presentation — a gray design
that resembles an electronic switchboard.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:50.460
"It also, seriously, schedules a therapy session
with a nearby professional so we can make

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.610
sure these sad feelings never, ever, ever
return."

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.191
"May I say something, Mr. Isherwell?"

00:05:56.191 --> 00:05:57.191
"No."

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:02.160
In addition to creating a disarming physical
look for the part, Rylance's demeanor also

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.010
manages to capture the hellish essence of
the character, which was originally inspired

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.979
by the mythological character Charon, the
ferryman who took souls down the river Styx

00:06:11.979 --> 00:06:12.979
in Hades.

00:06:12.979 --> 00:06:17.020
If he wasn't shudder-inducing enough with
his talent for predicting people's behavior

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:21.770
with the swipe of a hand, his disarmingly
droll delivery of Isherwell's most loaded

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.569
lines should do the trick.

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:29.470
One of the funniest bits in "Don't Look Up"
involves the accidental accuracy of Isherwell's

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.660
algorithm machine.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:35.560
Yes, his company, Bash, totally fails at the
guessing game when it comes to how well their

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:41.000
drones will work at breaking apart a planet-killing
comet, but one thing his machines do get exactly

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.720
right is the fate of President Orlean, even
several thousand years ahead of her end.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.110
Isherwell does misread the fate of another
character in the film: Dr. Mindy.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.080
During an argument over the Bash comet-busting
bonanza, Isherwell cruelly tells Mindy that

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.759
his formulas are so sophisticated that he's
already determined from data points that the

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:03.009
scientist will die alone.

00:07:03.009 --> 00:07:07.660
Mindy's recent history might suggest Isherwell
is right, since the astronomer left his wife

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.819
and children for the mostly uncaring TV personality
Brie.

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:15.510
But Isherwell has forgotten a major factor
in his equation.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:20.009
Unlike Orlean, who goes right along with the
predetermined trajectory that sees her abandon

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:25.520
her son and the planet she's doomed, only
to become dinner for a giant bird, Mindy actually

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.251
cares about his family.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.910
The title of "Don't Look Up" might refer to
the final ridiculous controversy that befalls

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:37.889
mankind before the comet arrives, but the
phrase also takes on another meaning in the

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:40.220
last few moments of the film.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.800
Once it's clear that their work trying to
inform the public is for naught, Randall,

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.659
Kate, and Dr. Oglethorpe meet with Yule and
Dr. Mindy's family in Michigan, where they

00:07:48.659 --> 00:07:53.039
turn off the news to enjoy one last supper
together in peace.

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:56.819
Although they've spent most of their time
together discussing the apocalypse ahead,

00:07:56.819 --> 00:08:01.610
they all finally decide to look down, literally
and figuratively.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:06.199
During their final moments, instead of watching
the sky like everyone else in the world, they

00:08:06.199 --> 00:08:10.449
keep their eyes lowered so they can look at
each other and talk about everything from

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:14.599
store-bought salmon to Mindy's obsession with
home-ground coffee beans.

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:19.349
Despite months spent encouraging folks to
look up, once they realize the futility of

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:24.669
that fight, they finally turn away from the
sky and embrace distraction, just like everyone

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:27.409
else has been doing the whole time.

00:08:27.409 --> 00:08:28.420
Thanks for watching.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:31.330
